// COURSE_CATALOG — course descriptions from Populi syllabi
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
const COURSE_CATALOG = {
  "BC 520": "Students will learn to assess operational performance within an organization and diagnose inhibitors to organizational effectiveness. Recognizing the role leadership plays in the effectiveness of the organization, students will learn to work collaboratively with them to develop a system-based approach to organizational development.",
  "BL 212": "This Survey course will include core components for building a foundational understanding of the New Testament that will enrich more in-depth courses of study later. The first component will explore NT backgrounds from the Intertestamental Period beginning with the Greek Empire. A second component will focus on the introducing the Gospels and the life of Christ in particular. A third component will discover the early church launch and development, with key focus on the ministries and writings of Peter and Paul. The final component will review the final books of the New Testament, emphasizing the ministry and writings of the Apostle John.",
  "BL 342 -B": "Course Description:\n\nThis course will focus on the life of Paul the Apostle and his New Testament writings to churches and individuals. To gain full understanding, these epistles will be studied in chronological sequence and in the historical settings of their writing. Each letter will be correlated with Paul’s missionary journeys, activities, and personnel; to ensure complete awareness of contextual matters. This approach should enable correct interpretation of these first-century documents and enhance proper application for churches and believers today. \n\nSpecific Learning Outcomes:\n\nUpon completion of this course, the learner should be able to:\n\n1.\tDepict a biographical portrait of the Apostle Paul including his early years and training; his personality and passionate nature; his radical transformation and ministry preparation; and his total life commitment to fulfill God’s Calling.\n\n2.\tDenote key events and experiences in Paul’s life: including his missionary journeys and ministry ventures; opposition and challenges; ministry accomplishments and life victories.\n\n3.\tDescribe the location and date, audience, and purpose for the writing of each Pauline Epistle, as well as, noting key characteristics and features for each one.\n\n4.\tDefine key theological concepts within each of Paul’s Letters, core themes seen across multiple epistles, and their relevancy for Christians today. \n\n5.\tDemonstrate an accurate understanding of the most challenging passages in Paul’s Epistles and the ability to give a clear and reasonable explanation in response. \n\n6.\tDetermine personal life-change possibilities based on application of biblical truths discovered from studying Paul’s life and ministry, and the NT Epistles he wrote.",
  "CM 489": "This course is designed to help students consolidate the key principles of general education, biblical knowledge, and Christian ministry. Students will answer questions that integrate and synthesize key principles of biblical understanding, biblical worldview, spiritual transformation, communication of truth, and leadership and ministry principles. A minimum grade of C in this course is required for graduation.",
  "COA 520": "Students will learn to assess different leadership styles and implement a compelling process to help leaders get better outcomes.  When working with teams, the student will be equipped to assess and build essential behaviors to increase efficiencies and effectiveness.",
  "ECON 101": "In ECON 101, free-market thought will be evaluated from the Christian perspective. The supply \nand demand model analyzes microeconomics issues, including market structures, government \nregulations, labor market, financial market, and trade. \n \nAlso, this course teaches the student about the structure of the economy and the interactions \namong its different sectors so that they can make sound economic decisions, whether for self \nor public benefit.",
  "ENG 103": "An exercise in the ability to read and think critically and to write persuasively. A study of the collection and synthesis of information from a variety of sources, focusing on the ability to use such information to logically construct an argument.",
  "MIS 310": "A consideration of the dimensions and manifestations of culture as they influence ministry, with special attention given to specific skills for exegeting a cultural setting for ministry.  May be taken as dual credit for General Education, Social Science.",
  "OL 500": "This course is designed to provide a framework for gathering and evaluating the challenges leaders face globally.  The need for distinguishing between fact and fiction; sound principles and external biases while developing solid strategies for addressing local, national and global issues has never been greater.  Students will be challenged to assess their basis for analyzing information that comes to them and understand the biases of various sources of information in their analysis.  They will develop an organized, systematic approach for critically filtering the bombardment of data coming to them as they lead their respective organizations.",
  "OL 560": "Course Description:\n\nThis course develops the leader’s ability to create and effectively communicate a strategic vision within an organization and understand an individual’s impact on others. Students learn to identify and improve self-awareness of communication and conflict styles and abilities, and to engage, influence, and motivate employees. Topics will include creating a unified organizational climate and culture, managing conflict, understanding team dynamics, and building strong and enduring teams.\n\n\nSpecific Learning Outcomes:\n\nThe student will: \n\n1.\tUnderstand the principles and complexities of effectively communicating within an organization’s culture\n2.\tBe able to use various techniques of communication with people and groups\n3.\tBe able to evaluate the effectiveness of their communication skills within the organization\n4.\tUnderstand the reasons and purposes of organizational and interpersonal conflict\n5.\tBe able to use various approaches to preventing and resolving conflict\n6.\tKnow how to recover from conflict\n7.\tBe able to explain how leadership effectiveness is tied to effective communication.",
  "ORI 101": "This course is designed to provide students with a thorough introduction to the College, including policies and procedures, academic expectations and financial matters. Focus will be given to time management and study skills that will enhance the student’s opportunity for academic success. The course will also include information about the use of technology in the classroom. This course meets for two four-hour sessions. All degree-seeking students must complete Orientation with a passing grade during the first six months of enrollment and before they register for courses that begin after that period of time.\n\n           Specific Learning Outcomes\n\nUpon completion of this course, the student will:\n1. Demonstrate an understanding of College policies.\n2. Demonstrate an understanding of strategies for success in their College work.\n3. Meet various College faculty members as well as staff members working in the business office, financial aid and the library.\n4. Demonstrate an understanding of online research tools.\n5. Demonstrate an understanding of principles of good writing.\n6. Demonstrate an understanding of the Christian Service requirement.\n7. Become familiar with the process of taking hybrid/blended courses.\n8. Become familiar with ways to receive credit by other means, including Credit by\nDemonstrated Competency and College Level Examination Program (CLEP).\n\nOffice Hours:",
  "PD 101": "A study of the setting of goals, as well as the priorities necessary to attain these goals, with an emphasis upon how goals and priorities relate to the realities and aspirations of life and the workplace. The attitude of the individual including other aspects of psychological makeup and how they impact the ability and willingness to set goals and establish priorities will be discussed.",
  "WOR 315": "An overview of the “personal devotion practice” of Jesus– looking at these practices as 1) an incarnated reflection of the essence of God’s mandated ‘practices’ for Israel’s worship, 2) as a reflection of the ‘great worship-focus’ shift He inaugurated (from external to internal, e.g. John 4: 22-24); and evaluate seven (7) exercises that will help students develop a more consistent ‘companioning worship walk’ Christ for themselves.",
  "ACCT 310": "This course focuses on the fundamental principles of finances. The basic knowledge, concepts, and terms of the discipline are appropriate for all students. Biblical concepts of sound financial decision making will be covered as it relates to the current economy both from a personal and business point of view.",
  "BL 201": "This course is a study of the early history of God’s people in the Old Testament as recorded from Genesis through Solomon. We will begin with an exploration of the Pentateuch, the five books of Moses, Genesis through Deuteronomy. Then, the History books Joshua through Ruth will be reviewed. Samuel will transition us to the United Kingdom of Saul, David, and Solomon. Most of the Poetic Books, known as the Wisdom Literature of the Old Testament, were written as this time; so, they will also be included in the content for this course.   \n\n\n             Specific Learning Outcomes\n\nThe student will:\n\n1.\tDepict background influences that shaped the context of the Biblical world from Creation to United Monarch, including historical, geographical, cultural, political, and religious backgrounds and conditions in the Near East and dominant World Civilizations.\n\n2.\tDemonstrate an understanding of the history and chronology of God’s early covenants with humanity and the Jewish people and explain how these primary influences impact the content of the Old Testament Scriptures and an understanding of His History of Redemption.\n\n3.\tDistinguish the similarities and differences of the Pentateuch, Historical, and Poetic literary genres, including characteristics &amp; features, form &amp; function, and interpretation of these types of Old Testament writings.\n\n4.\tDepict God-given roles and examples of key Covenant leaders as well as other priests, judges, prophets, and kings.\n\n5.\tDetermine personal life-change possibilities based on application of biblical truths discovered from reviewing all the content in this Old Testament Survey course.\n\nINSTRUCTOR OFFICE HOURS:",
  "BL 388": "This course includes 12 weeks of pre-trip classes that will be taken in tandem with BL 389 Israel Biblical Study Trip which is a two week journey throughout Israel.",
  "CT 325": "An introduction to theology from a systematic approach. This course will especially focus on the doctrines of the Bible; Theology Proper (God the Father, Trinity); Angels, Satan, Demons; Man; and Sin.",
  "MAT 210": "NOTE: A course meeting date falls on a holiday (1/19), and will meet in person to make up this class on 1/22 in the Conference Room. All other class meetings will be held on site in the Library.\n\nThe Business Mathematics course is designed to prepare today’s students with practical, contemporary math skills they will need to build their future career success in the world of business and economics.  With today’s global, fast progress and breakthroughs in business technology, success in personal matters and business employment presents the basic need to understand the fundamental principles of business math.  The goal of the course is to provide realistic skill-building business mathematics through step-by-step development of concepts, numerous practice exercises, and business application of mathematical techniques. The text is easy to read and the exercises are relevant to many real-life activities.  The entire study is structured to meet the needs of students in the liberal arts.\n\n      STUDENT LEARNING OUTCOMES\n\nUpon completion of this course, the student will:\n 1 - Demonstrate an understanding of the terminology and the methods of\nworking with fractions and decimals.\n2 - Demonstrate the ability to compute sales commissions and gross pay,\ngraduated sales commissions, and sales and purchases for principals.\n3 - Demonstrate the ability to compute trade discounts, equivalent single\ndiscount rate for a series of trade discounts, discounts and remittance amounts for fully and partially paid invoices, and markup.\n4 -  Demonstrate the ability to compute the variables in the basic markup\nformula, understand and compute costs and savings for auto, property, life, and\nmedical insurance premiums, contributions, and reimbursements and simple interest.\n5 - Understand and compute the most basic concepts of business finance of\ninstallment purchases, promissory notes, and discounting.\n6 - Comprehend the personal use of compound interest, international currency\nexchange rates, the effect of exchange rate changes and compute duties on imports.\n7 - Understand and compute business statistics which includes the collection,\norganization, analysis, and presentation of data.",
  "OL 501": "This course is an examination and analysis of the qualities and practices of the effective Christian leader, particularly across cultural boundaries.  This study will be based on principles in Scripture and related literature, with attention to devising a personal philosophy of intercultural leadership.",
  "PD 100": "This course is designed to develop strong skills used in academic subjects to foster success in all future coursework. It will focus on competency in the implementation of strategies for increasing self-awareness, enhancing study and time management skills, increasing reading comprehension, employing rules of grammar and usage, completing assignments, successfully preparing for and taking tests, engaging in ethical use of research materials and artificial intelligence, writing and formatting essays and projects, speaking and presenting effectively, and developing positive relationships and communicating effectively with fellow students, professors, and administration. Students will respond to oral and written questions after reading passages. Students will also prepare a persuasive essay and presentation for their final project.  By the end of the course, students will have a personalized toolkit for success at Williamson College and beyond.",
  "ACCT 301": "ACCT 301 is a foundation course in principles of accounting designed primarily for accounting or business students as part of integrated business education. It is also helpful for any major who wishes to understand the language of business. \n \nIn ACCT 301, students will study basic transactions, general ledger accounts, books of original entry, closing and adjusting entry processes, trial balances, financial statements, accounting for assets, liabilities, sole proprietorship, equity, revenues, and expenses. \n \n                      Learning Outcomes \nUpon successful completion of this course, the student will be able to: \n\n1.\tCorrectly apply terminology used in accounting, which is the language of business. \n2.\tAnalyze business transactions to record these business transactions in the accounting records according to generally accepted accounting principles. \n3.\tDemonstrate his/her ability to complete the accounting cycle for both service and merchandise entities. \n4.\tCompile and analyze the four basic financial statements—Income Statement, Statement of Owner’s Equity, Balance Sheet, and Statement of Cash Flows—according to generally accepted accounting principles. \n5.\tAnalyze and record transactions related to stocks, bonds, and investments.",
  "BUS 421": "The introduction to concepts in the development of friends and funds to support the mission and vision of the nonprofit organization.  This class will consider the annual fund, major gifts, special events, and donor data development and tracking.",
  "CM 303": "This course is a study of New Testament discipleship principles and their application, with a special emphasis upon building discipling relationships and small group ministry. Groups are the building blocks of organizations and Christian ministries.  Nearly everyone participates in both formal and informal groups.  Leaders must understand group process because it directly affects creativity, problem solving, decision making and productivity.  This course focuses primarily on the principles of discipleship and the function of small groups in a church setting, with an understanding that many of the principles also apply to other areas of life that are comprised of groups of people.\n\n                      Learning Outcomes\nWhen students have completed this course, they will be able to: \n1. Understand the biblical basis and foundational principles for discipleship and how it is employed through small groups within the church.\n2.  Describe the use of learning methodologies in varying discipleship contexts and how these are applied through current models of discipleship. \n3. Examine the role and influence of small groups to provide the basis for engagement in Scripture, evangelism, ministry, and biblical community that provides opportunity for discipleship growth, the discovery spiritual gifts, and to empower individuals and organizations to reach potential.\n4. Compare and contrast various methodologies of deploying small groups within the framework of organizations and understand their impact.\n5. Recognize the value of how starting groups encourages the health of the church and further multiplication of disciples and recognize what conflicts can arise within small groups.",
  "ENG 102": "This course is an exercise in critical reading and critical thinking, with a focus on effective strategies of persuasion.  It is a study of paragraph and composition structure, focusing on expository and persuasive writing as well as the rhetorical modes used individually and cooperatively to engage an audience through various forms of text.",
  "HIS 101": "A study of the foundations of the modern world from the first civilizations through the 18th century. Topics include Western and non-Western classical civilizations, the Middle Ages and the age of absolutism.",
  "LD 301": "A study of the biblical concepts of leadership in the Scripture and application of these concepts to personal, professional and career goals. The course will include the use of a survey to assess each individual's leadership capabilities to help him/her to understand his/her personal profile.",
  "LD 472": "Case studies will be selected for in-depth study of the application of leadership concepts with particular reference to those concepts found in Scripture. Students will develop a written analysis of their style of leadership. Related issues are introduced through readings.",
  "MGT 301": "A study of the five parts of managing organizations—planning, organizing, staffing, leading and controlling/evaluation—with the study of principles for application to both not-for-profit and for-profit organizations with references to Scripture.\n\n             Specific Learning Outcomes\nThe student will be able to do the following:\n1. Describe the four essential managerial tasks (planning, organizing, controlling, and leading), and demonstrate how they integrate to form a complete management structure.\n2. Summarize why Biblical ethics and morals promote the highest level of managerial accountability, and how they can be enforced cross-culturally and internationally.\n3. Describe how a leader casts an organizational vision and mission, and how to apply these principles toward the establishment of specific goals.\n4. Create a leadership model of communication, org culture management, and innovation.\n5. Create and lead a management-level business model for a new organization based on the four essential managerial tasks.",
  "MGT 311": "Creativity, opportunity, leadership, excellence and profit combined into an entrepreneurial approach to achieve the mission in small business.",
  "MGT 421": "This course includes reading, discussion and development of papers pertaining to relevant case studies and readings involving ethical issues applied to actual situations.",
  "OL 575": "This course is designed to train leaders how to think and plan strategically and to develop budgetary structure and discipline that will enable an organization to implement strategic plans.  Students will be equipped with practical instruction and resources for the development of sound strategic plans, budgetary forecasts, and the establishment of financial metrics to gauge effectiveness.",
  "ACCT 302": "This course covers basic financial accounting principles for a business or organization. Topics include the accounting cycle and the components of the preparation of financial statements. Students will learn how to prepare and analyze carious types of financial statements, as well as transactions unique to merchandising operations and inventory.",
  "BL 202": "This course is a study of the history of God’s people in the Old Testament from the Divided Monarchy through the Post-Babylonian Captivity. The foundation for the course will be the chronological sequence of the Kings through Esther Historical books. Isaiah through Malachi, known as Old Testament Prophets, also prophesied during this time; so these books will be integrated into the content for this course.",
  "BL 341": "A study of the Acts of the Apostles specifically following the historical expansion of the Church through doctrinal development, growth in understanding, evangelism and geographical expansion through missionary activities of its members. We will become acquainted with the activities of the great church leaders of the first century, i.e., Peter, James and Paul.\n\n            Specific Learning Outcomes\nUpon completion of this course, the learner should be able to:\n1. Describe the story line of the book of Acts\n2. Portray the contributions of each of the key early church leaders\n3. Explain the important aspects of the book of Acts\n4. Articulate its place in the Missio Dei\n5. Identify and explain significant doctrinal lessons presented in the book of Acts",
  "BUS 422": "A continuation in the study of concepts related to the development of friends and funds in the nonprofit organization.  This class will focus on grant writing, capital campaign design and implementation and planned giving.",
  "CT 321": "A course designed to equip students to be able to discuss and defend the essential truth-claims of Christianity with an emphasis on its basis in history, fact and reason.  The overall theme is “evidence for faith” and will address classic apologetics topics such as the relationship between faith and reason, evidence for the existence of God, cosmology, the problem of evil, the divinity of Christ, evidence for the resurrection, the reliability of the Bible, life after death as well as Christianity and other religions.",
  "IT 501": "Cybersecurity is a computing-based discipline involving technology, people, information, and processes to enable safe and secure IT operations in the presence of competitors and enemies. This course focuses on technical foundations that underlies discussions related to cybersecurity. Key concepts such as authentication, authorization, cryptography, CIA model, APT are explained and discussed. Class discussions are enhanced by hands-on exercises with cutting-edge cybersecurity tools such as Wireshark, nmap, OWASP Zed Attack Proxy (ZAP), Metasploit.",
  "LD 200": "This course is designed to provide a framework for gathering and evaluating the challenges leaders face globally.  The need for distinguishing between fact and fiction; sound principles and external biases while developing solid strategies for addressing local, national and global issues has never been greater.  Students will be challenged to assess their basis for analyzing information that comes to them and understand the biases of various sources of information in their analysis.  They will develop an organized, systematic approach for critically filtering the bombardment of data coming to them as they lead their respective organizations.",
  "MGT 441": "This course will explore the various risks that businesses face daily. Dealing with Business Risk (BR) can be challenging for those who do not have a reliable moral and ethical foundation; hence, a Biblical basis for evaluating BR is emphasized throughout the course. Among the topics studied are contract law, insurance risk (warranties and indemnification), human resources and employment law, corporate finance, cybersecurity, operational risks, etc. Managing the tension between risk, innovation, and investing in various organizations will prepare the student for successful managerial leadership.",
  "MGT 489": "NOTE: This course&#039;s Zoom link is shared with MGT 489 Capstone due to limited Zoom room availability. Please contact the office if a conflict arises between the two capstone project meetings.\n\nThis course is designed to help students consolidate the key principles of general education (including biblical worldview), biblical knowledge, and business administration by synthesizing the relationships between significant concepts and student experience. Students will prepare a paper, answering questions that integrate and synthesize key business principles of biblical worldview, spiritual transformation, communication of truth, leadership and ministry principles. This course is taught individually with each student focusing on their interests, experiences, and goals. A minimum grade of C in this course is required for graduation.",
  "OL 510": "This course is a survey of the development of ethical thought, both ancient and modern. Various ethical systems and their proponents, including alternative Christian perspectives, will be covered along with a discussion of contemporary ethical problems.",
  "PSI 115/116": "A study of earth science including physical and historical geology, meteorology, and descriptive astronomy. The economic, social, and philosophic aspects of the subject matter will be explored. The course includes lectures and demonstrations.\n\nThe lab is primarily designed to be a series of experiential learning exercises that can be done individually and submitted either from an onsite, distance or online course format. The focus is intended to be based on flexibility, with the freedom for students to make choices based on their learning styles and what they are interested in learning more about. The aim of the lab is to provide some direction toward meaningful learning and lesson application. The goal of the lab is to provide an engaging and enjoyable experience for adult learners.\n\n          Specific Learning Outcomes\nStudents will gain an understanding of basic scientific concepts relating to the Earth and how Earth science is relevant to daily life. Willing students will learn how to apply the scientific principles and interrelationships as we:\n1. Geology: Summarize the study of geology from a historical standpoint. Compare and contrast uniformitarianism and catastrophism; state your opinion on how and why we\nshould continue to study our earth as Christians.\n2. Summarize the principles of the Hydroplate theory vs. plate tectonics or continental drift including the effects of past mega sequences on the geological makeup of the Earth today including the cause, nature and historical effects of earthquakes, tsunamis, and volcanoes.\n3. Summarize the current climate change argument and give your opinion on how we as Christians should respond to this argument. Make sure to support your opinion with at least 4 pieces of evidence.\n4. Summarize the significance of the discovery of the wooly mammoths and explain how this strengthens the Hydroplate theory. Give at least 4 pieces of evidence that support your\nexplanation.\n5. Astronomy: Explain how it is possible for us to know and defend that our solar\nsystem is young. Give at least 4 pieces of evidence that support your explanation.",
  "BL 331": "A study of the Gospels focusing upon the life and ministry of Jesus Christ and His death and resurrection with a view toward applying His life-changing principles to one's everyday life.",
  "CT 301": "A study of the Biblical worldview as presented in the Old and New Testaments in contrast to alternate worldviews. Use of texts and Scripture will be employed to explore the roles of General Revelation (creation and morality) and Special Revelation (the Scriptures and Jesus Christ) to apprehend the truths of God as well as equip the learner to integrate faith and learning and to experience God at work in today’s world.",
  "CT 421": "This course is an examination of religious belief systems found in the major world religions. The functional goal is to understand such belief systems in a biblical context in order to develop effective evangelistic strategies.",
  "MGT 371": "Managers are only effective when they understand themselves well. Once they have insights into their own mental constructs, then they can influence others effectively. This course explores 1) the manager as a person, 2) influencing and managing different types of people, 3) how to manage others regardless of organizational position, and 4) leading teams effectively. We begin with understanding yourself as a potential manager, then we move on to management theories regarding motivation, personal growth, and effective communication. We also explore conflict, generational issues in the workplace, and gender issues. \n\nMGT 371 gives the student a solid working knowledge of all these topics, while providing useful, practical strategies and tips to be an effective manager and leader.",
  "OL 530": "This course is designed to create an understanding of the underlying patterns, values, \ntraditions, assumptions, and behaviors, both espoused and concealed, that make up what is \nconsidered an organizational culture.  Every organization’s culture is distinct and this course \nintends to guide students in effective analysis of an organization’s culture for the purpose of \nmanaging the culture toward desired behavioral change and goal achievement.  The course is \nsuitable in analyzing for-profit, nonprofit, and church cultures.",
  "SPT 243": "NOTE: A class meeting for this course falls on a holiday (1/19). It is up to the instructor to reschedule this class. MAKE UP CLASSES ARE ALWAYS VIA ZOOM, and the instructor has the option to hold the class on the scheduled holiday or move it to Tuesday 1/20 or Wednesday 1/21 of the same week.)\n\nThe principles of speech composition, outlining and delivery are discussed. There is practice in preparing and presenting speeches that can inform, persuade, demonstrate, and actuate. The student will be encouraged to make immediate application of principles within the current work environment.\n\n                          Learning Outcomes\n\nUpon completion of this course, students will:\n1.   Develop public speaking skills for their personal, professional, and community lives.\n2.   Understand the meaning and importance of audience-centered communication.\n3.   Understand the “How can I serve you?” perspective.\n4.   Recognize, explain, and apply the key principles involved in preparing to speak.\n5.   Understand and apply strategies regarding how to develop and organize appropriate content and to support it with relevant, specific, and appropriate evidence.\n6.   Recognize, understand, and apply effective speech delivery skills/strategies.\n7.   Identify and explain the basic features/strategies of the various forms of public speaking.\n8.   Describe, apply, and explain the importance and uses of the basic elements of active listening.\n9.   Understand how to use basic presentational aids effectively and be able to choose the most appropriate forms for any given speaking situation.\n10.  Recognize individual apprehension regarding public speaking and apply appropriate strategies to overcome it.\n11.  Apply strategies to increase self-confidence and personal motivation to present to others.\n12.   Explain the basic concept of ethos and apply various strategies to enhance speaker credibility.",
  "BUS 431": "An overview of various communication requirements, styles and medium that are critical in the development of a shared vision among stakeholders of the nonprofit organization.",
  "ENG 311": "The course will explore the life and literature of C. S. Lewis to gain insight into his theology, the various genres in which he wrote, and the principles that make his writings meaningful to us today. Moreover, I would like you to appreciate his writing more. Hopefully, this will serve as a springboard to your continued reading of C.S. Lewis!\n\n                    Learning Outcomes\n\nStudents will be able to:\n*Identify individual genres of literature used by C. S. Lewis\n\n*Describe the relationship between Lewis and J.R.R. Tolkien. Describe their influence and impact on each other and their writings.\n\n*Identify why Lewis wrote what he did. What was his motivation to write each of the books we studied? \n\n*Understand the themes Lewis promotes in his writing.\n\n*Develop a much deeper understanding and appreciation of Lewis as a person from his biography.",
  "MGT 381": "Organizational goals, priorities and strategies interfacing with the behavior of individuals and groups inside the organization and in other affecting organizations. Attention given to the effect of organizational culture, government laws and regulations and the economy upon organizational behavior.",
  "MIS 305": "A study of the biblical foundations and purposes of missions, the nature of the missionary vocation, and the authority and scope of the New Testament in relation to missions.",
  "OL 540": "This course is an examination of change and its relationship to conflict within an organization.  This course will focus on enabling leaders to understand, anticipate, and direct change as well as how to handle criticism in their organizations.\n\n                LEARNING OUTCOMES\n\nAt the conclusion of this course, students should be able to:\n1.\tUnderstand the role that leadership and culture play in change management and how to develop strategies that utilize both.\n2.\tLearn how to use successful change as the starting point for future change.\n3.\tIntegrate the essential factors of buy-in, flexibility, follow-up, and finishing into the change process.\n4.\tCreate a mission/values/vision foundation for organizational change.\n5.\tDesign a comprehensive change process from inception to completion.\n\nINSTRUCTORS&#039; OFFICE HOURS:",
  "OL 590": "This course is designed to teach leadership, utilizing a biblical lens, through examples of cultural, political, and business dynamics in the countries of Belgium and United Kingdom. Topics will include cross-cultural communication, conflict management and resolution, and the effect of contemporary decisions based on biblical precedence. Lessons in leadership will be taught through instruction, discussion, interviews, visits to contemporary and historic sites, and biblical study.",
  "WOR 221": "A study of the basic issues of the Theology of Worship; starting with God’s revelation of Himself through Creation, to His worship training through the establishment of the Sacrificial System given to Israel, to an evaluation of the worship life of Jesus, and on into the development of worship instruction given in the New Testament epistles and the Revelation of John.",
  "BC 501": "This course is divided into two sections.  In the first section, students will be introduced to corporate finance with an emphasis on understanding financial statements. In the second section, students will learn the importance and purpose of the consulting engagement process and the concepts and theories common to the consulting industry.",
  "BL 301": "A study of the Old and New Testaments along with the hermeneutical principles necessary for their proper interpretation. There will also be emphasis on the process through which a person proceeds in his/her quest for faith and the practical principles needed to apply first-century truth to twenty-first-century life.",
  "BL 389": "Taken with BL 388, this course is a two week journey throughout Israel, having class at the core Old Testament, Inter-testamental, and New Testament biblical sites.  Students explore the social, religious, economic, political, and spiritual condition of OT/NT Israel as well as modern-day Israel.",
  "COA 501": "This course is divided into two sections.  In the first section, students will be introduced to corporate finance with an emphasis on understanding financial statements. Understanding financial statements will give the student familiarity with the common financial terminology used by executives regarding their company’s performance.\nIn the second section, students will learn the fundamentals of operating a successful coaching practice, including the fundamentals of coaching, developing a successful coaching model, and the importance of contracting.\nIn the second section, students will learn the fundamentals of operating a successful coaching practice, including the fundamentals of coaching, developing a successful coaching model, and the importance of contracting.",
  "CT 410": "A thorough examination of the doctrine of Scripture, including revelation, inspiration, illumination, and inerrancy.",
  "MGT 361": "This course focuses on building relationships which is the key to a successful sales career. The eight step sales cycle is studied and learned.  Attitude, one of the building blocks for achieving success in selling, verbal and nonverbal communication and social style make up the some of the other concepts covered in this course. Tying these concepts into a Biblical perspective is critical for the professional sales person who wants to conduct themselves in an honest and ethical manner.",
  "OL 599": "This is a leadership project, selected by the student, which will pertain to a particular opportunity or problem in their organization or ministry. The purpose of the Capstone is to help the student apply the elements of organizational leadership which the student acquired in the program.  The development of this project will be conducted throughout the program and will be guided by a professor in the program.",
  "CT 403": "This is a study of complexities of our modern world and how we live in it as Christians. This course will demonstrate that Christian ethics is ultimately rooted in the nature and actions of God and in the worldview derived from the biblical story. A biblical worldview acts as the basis for decision-making when facing ethical issues of life.",
  "OL 550": "This course is a special seminar that will consider the particular opportunities and challenges of leading at the national government and corporate levels.  Leaders from these contexts will predominate the course as guest speakers.  The course examines aspects of managing a national business or ministry, with particular focus on ethical dilemmas, legal and political considerations, and competitive market forces.",
  "PHI 213": "This course will develop an understanding of the philosophical concepts, beginning with the Greeks through Augustine and Aquinas, concluding with the significant philosophical systems of the Western world and Eastern philosophy. Evaluation of these systems relative to personal faith and values will assist the individual student in developing a statement of philosophy.",
  "BC 510": "Understanding the dynamic relationships with leaders, managers, and technicians is a critical skill to the effective consultant.  This course will help the student unpack the complexities associated with the most common leadership and management styles, learn to effectively address conflict within the organization, and assess the impact these disciplines have on company culture, overall company performance, and the consulting relationship.",
  "COA 510": "At one point or another, most people will find themselves vacillating between roles of leadership, management, and technician.  In the course, the student will learn to identify appropriate coaching tools and develop techniques to help clients reach their goals within each of these roles as well as the transition between them.  The course will also equip the student to help their clients achieve a sustainable balance between their business and personal life.",
  "MIS 300": "An introductory survey of major missiological motifs. The biblical foundation for missions, theological ramifications of cross-cultural communication of the Gospel, strategies for applied missiology, and the historical expansion of Christian missions are all of major concern. The historical survey highlights the modern mission era and draws attention to trends which will shape missionary activity in the 21st century.",
  "CT 420": "A study of the existence and attributes of the one God, the Holy Trinity and the how this doctrine should be central to the life of the believer and the local church.  Emphasis will be placed on attacks and defense of Trinitarianism.",
  "CT 330": "Course Description\n\nUsing a systematic theology approach, this course will focus on the second five of the ten major doctrines of the Bible. Christology, the doctrine of Christ, will be the first theme addressed; followed by Soteriology, including provision and application of Salvation. Pneumatology will consider the person and work of the Holy Spirit, in conjunction with Ecclesiology, the doctrine of the New Testament Church. Eschatology will be the final doctrine studied, including all key Future Events.\n\n\nSpecific Learning Outcomes\n\nUpon completion of this course the student will be able to:\n\n1.\tDefine Systematic Theology in comparison and contrast to other approaches to theological studies. \n\n2.\tDescribe doctrinal truths and scriptural references for the person and work of Jesus Christ.\n\n3.\tDenote doctrinal truths and scriptural references for the provision for and the application of Salvation.\n\n4.\tDemonstrate doctrinal truths and scriptural references for the person and work of the Holy Spirit.\n\n5.\tDepict doctrinal truths and scriptural references to the nature and mission of God’s New Testament Church.\n\n6.\tDesignate doctrinal truths and scriptural references to all key Future Events.\n\n7.\tDetermine personal life-change possibilities based on application of biblical truths discovered from studying the five systematic theology themes of this course.",
  "CT 430": "A study of God’s grace in salvation, including the humiliation and exaltation of the Savior, the role of the Holy Spirit, the nature and extent of the atonement, regeneration, justification, and glorification.",
  "CT 440": "A study of the doctrines of the body of Christ and the local church.  Emphasis will be placed on its organization, ministry, ordinances, polity, and purpose.",
  "MGT 431": "This course will focus on ethical issues and concepts in the workplace.  Specific attention will be given to challenges surrounding ethical decisions with and without a presupposition of a transcendent reality (ultimate truth in God).  Biblical concepts for ethical living will undergird the analysis and discussion. This course also includes reading, discussion and development of papers pertaining to relevant case studies and readings involving ethical issues applied to actual situations.",
  "CT 251": "This course is designed to prepare students in thinking biblically in all of life’s situations. Utilizing Alpha International as a framework, students will explore various aspects of the Christian Faith, including discussions that consider life’s biggest questions.",
  "HIS 102": "A study of the major world events from the age of revolution to the present. Topics include the French Revolution, the cults of the 19th century, the world wars of the 20th century and the world today.",
  "MKT 361": "Managers and their understanding of the role of marketing for an organization. Emphasis will be given to the factors that affect consumer behavior, the development and evaluation of an organization's marketing strategies, and the fundamental marketing variables.",
  "CT 501": "A seminar focused on understanding the metanarrative of the Bible through various theological themes and how those themes also appear within localized stories in the Bible. Various frameworks and thinkers emphasizing both the Old Testament and the New Testament will be studied. This course will focus less on analyzing attributes of God and more on exegeting and applying interpretive theological lenses to the text. Moreover, it will train the student on how to do biblical theology",
  "CT 201": "This course is a study of the history of the Church from its founding on the day of Pentecost to the present time. This course will emphasize the development of Christian doctrine during the last 2,000 years as well as the role of the Church in proclaiming, articulating, and defending it. The course may be taken as dual credit for HIS elective.",
  "LD 211": "From the Academic Catalog: “This course explores the foundations of a Biblical world view to help the student understand sound principles of critical thinking and personal financial decisions and management.  The bible has much to say about our attitudes toward money and wealth management that will help believers through the most difficult times and to understand true contentment and to be “wise as serpents and harmless as doves.”  Matthew 10:16.",
  "CT/OL 590": "This course is designed to teach leadership, utilizing a biblical lens, through examples of cultural, political, and business dynamics in the countries of Belgium, The Netherlands, and Germany. Topics will include cross-cultural communication, theology, and the effect of contemporary leadership decisions based on biblical precedence. Lessons in leadership and theology will be taught through instruction, discussion, interviews, visits to contemporary and historic sites, and biblical study.",
  "CT 510": "Systematic Theology 1 covers the resurrection of Jesus, the nature and product of revelation, questions about whether knowing God or about God is possible, arguments for God’s existence, and the major attributes of God, including but not limited to omnipotence, omniscience, omnipresence, omnibenevolence. Historical development about these attributes, philosophical helps for understanding these attributes, and key biblical themes influenced by these attributes will be considered. Key thinkers from any era who offered major contributions to the discussion will be considered.",
  "HIS 110": "This course is a survey of the political, social, and economic history of the North American region that became the United States from pre-European contact through the era of Reconstruction. Themes include Native American cultures and societies, European settlement, colonial British North America, the War for American Independence, nation-building, industrialization, slavery, western expansion, and the Civil War and Reconstruction.",
  "PSY 103": "A study of the fundamental concepts of psychology including biological processes and development, behavior, learning and memory, personality, psychological disorders and social psychology.",
  "SOC 201": "Course Description:\n\nA study of how humans interact in groups, of how socialization, culture, organizations, institutions, and change influence that social interaction, of how Christianity and sociology can be rather than antagonistic or mutually exclusive, and of how individuals, as Christians, may find their place and pursue their goals/life missions in contemporary society.",
  "CT 515": "A seminar designed to learn the philosophical discussions around major theological issues, e.g. freedom and determinism, personhood, attributes of God, natural theology, special revelation, miracles, evil and suffering, etc. The course will introduce frameworks such as perfect being theology for thinking about God and integrating all of God’s attributes.\n\n                   Learning Outcomes\n\nStudents will be able to:\n\n•\tArticulate a personal theological statement including the traditional attributes of God;\n•\tDemonstrate understanding of the basic philosophical categories and concepts;\n•\tEngage the Biblical data with appropriate and helpful philosophical tools;\n•\tDemonstrate the ability to engage with thinkers in the field, forming and critiquing arguments.",
  "CT 520": "Systematic Theology 2 covers theological/biblical anthropology, Christology, and soteriology. Historical development of these doctrines, philosophical insights into these doctrines, and biblical themes impacted by the doctrines will be considered. Key thinkers from any era who offered major contributions to the discussion will be considered with major emphasis on various theories of the atonement and their implications for the Christian life and worldview.",
  "PSI 116": "The lab is primarily designed to be a series of experiential learning exercises that can be done individually and submitted either from an onsite, distance or online course format. The focus is intended to be based on flexibility, with the freedom for students to make choices based on their learning styles and what they are interested in learning more about. The aim of the lab is to provide some direction toward meaningful learning and lesson application. The goal of the lab is to provide an engaging and enjoyable experience for adult learners.",
  "CT 530": "Systematic Theology 3 examines pneumatology, ecclesiology, and eschatology. This course will be less philosophical and focus more on the biblical insights and historical moves concerning these doctrines. Key thinkers from any era who offered major contributions to the discussion will be considered with major emphasis on an integration of the work of the Holy Spirit and the church.",
  "HIS 111": "This course is a survey of the political, social, and economic history of the United States since the Reconstruction Era. Themes include industrialization and its impacts, mass immigration, the changing role of the federal government, the rise of the United States as a world power, and the political, international, social, and cultural changes that have shaped contemporary America",
  "CT 540": "Course Description\n\nWhile generally a primary Roman Catholic mode of thinking, developing a strong ethical sense and framework informed and constructed by key theological attributes of God is crucial for anyone wanting to engage a culture of social justice, political justice, and the variegated, complex technological and likewise issues of the day. This course goes beyond a basic Introduction to Christian ethics by focusing upon the theological anchors, emphases, and implications of theology on ethical decisions and topics.\n\n                      Learning Outcomes\n\nStudents will be able to:\n\n•\tArticulate a personal statement of ethics that is theologically coherent and biblical;\n•\tDemonstrate understanding of the basic ethical categories and concepts;\n•\tEngage the Biblical data with appropriate and helpful ethical tools;\n•\tDemonstrate the ability to engage with thinkers in the field, forming and critiquing arguments.",
  "BL 200": "A study of the Old and New Testaments along with the hermeneutical principles necessary for their proper interpretation. There will also be emphasis on the process through which a person proceeds in his/her quest for faith and the practical principles needed to apply first-century truth to twenty-first-century life.",
  "BUS 501": "Entrepreneurs are willing to make difficult and complicated decisions for which most people are not willing to take responsibility.  The implications of those decisions result in the vital contributions of entrepreneurs to economic growth.  This course will provide the student with an introduction to entrepreneurship from the perspectives of arbitrage, innovation, and risk-taking.  Students will learn to collect information, make judgment calls, raise finances, and develop organizations.  Additionally, students will learn the impacts of multiple factors: the volatility of the business environment, market structure, attitudes to risk, the availability of capital, government policy, and the dynamics of social and cultural aspects of the environment.",
  "CT 550": "The Great Theologians seminar will focus on a key theological thinker who significantly impacted the church, the culture, and theological thinking subsequent to their contemporary era. Examples of theologians to be featured in the seminar are: Augustine, Aquinas, Barth, Schleiermacher, Edwards, Hodge, Warfield, Tillich, Calvin, Berkhof, etc. Whichever theologian is featured, their entire theological system including brief sketches of their biography will be analyzed, critiqued, and studied.",
  "CT 560": "Course Description:\n \nA seminar comparing, contrasting, and interacting with the key theological thinkers of other religious systems than Christianity. The student will be able to read primary sources from prominent scholars and practitioners in other religious systems and other faith traditions. This course will be structured in terms of religious worldviews. \n \nLearning Outcomes:\n \n1.\tStudents will understand the historical origins and development of religious systems other than Christianity. (reading; research paper)\n\n2.\tStudents will compare and contrast the beliefs of non-Christian religious systems with Christianity and their own faith tradition. (participation; reading; analysis)\n\n3.\tStudents will compare and contrast the practices of non-Christian religious systems with Christianity and their own faith tradition. (research paper; analysis)\n\n4.\tStudents will identify points of contact between non-Christian religious systems and Christianity and delineate the connection between them. (research paper; analysis) \n\n5.\tStudents will formulate a personal response to a religious system other than Christianity based on their own faith experience. (participation; research paper; book review)\n\n6.\tStudents will Identify and describe the historical origins, core beliefs and practices of selected world religions with accuracy and charity (Bloom: Remember / Understand).\n\n7.\tStudents will Explain biblical categories of idolatry, revelation, and worship as they relate to the religions of the world, including Daniel Strange’s concept of subversive fulfillment (Bloom: Understand).\n\n8.\tStudents will Compare and contrast non-Christian religious worldviews with biblically and historically orthodox Christian doctrine (Bloom: Analyze).\n\n9.\tStudents will Evaluate religious truth claims in light of Scripture and historic Christian theology (Bloom: Evaluate).\n\n10.\tStudents will Formulate a biblically faithful, pastorally wise, and missionally compelling approach to Christian witness in a religiously plural context (Bloom: Create).",
  "CT 570": "Focused on practical theology for the Christian life centered around the local church. Students will survey the various spiritual disciplines as they consider their own spiritual formation. The course will also examine matters concerning Christian leadership and the role of theological education for the church.",
  "CT/BL/CM 599": "Topic to be selected by student, which will pertain to a particular opportunity or problem in their organization or ministry. The purpose of the Capstone is to help the student apply and demonstrate the program goals taught throughout the program. The development of this project will be conducted throughout the program and will be guided by a professor in the program.",
  "BUS 510": "Entrepreneurs face many challenges when obtaining financing for new and emerging ventures. In many cases, securing outside funding is critical to the viability, sustainability, and growth of the firm.  This course will expose the student to the many challenges, returns, and risks with entrepreneurial financing as well as the many sources of capital, including angel investors, venture capital funds, private equity funds, initial public offerings, hedge funds, trade credit, factoring and discounting, leasing, banks, microfinance, and project finance.  Students will learn to assess the firm’s capital structure and its impact on profitability.",
  "BUS 520": "Setting the strategic direction for the business is the most complex task facing the leadership in the firm.  The complexity exists because the business strategy of a firm is forward-looking, which is unknown and unknowable, the dynamic environment in which they compete, and the plethora of choices in strategic paths available to leadership.  Adding to the complexity of crafting a business strategy is the people.  In this course, students will learn to craft, identify, assess, and execute the strategic decisions facing a firm in the present-day marketplace.  Students will also learn to recognize and navigate through the complexity of the individual views and motives of the leadership team, which may or may not be explicit, and the constraints present by their past experiences, assumptions, biases, and prejudices.",
  "IT 141": "This \nnon\n-\ntechnical \ncourse provides hands\n-\non experience using commercial software packages for business \napplications. Topics include microcomputer system components, \noperating systems, word processing, \nspreadsheets, and presentation software. Keyboarding\nskills are highly recommended.",
  "HIS/PS 201": "A close examination of America’s founding documents including their classical roots as well as the intersection of Enlightenment and Judeo-Christian thought in 18th century America.  The following are among the documents that will be reviewed:\n\n•\tThe Declaration of Independence\n·\tArticles of Confederation\n·\tThe U.S. Constitution\n·\tThe Bill of Rights\n·\tThe Federalist Papers\n·\tDocuments such as the Magna Carta, the Mayflower Compact, the Emancipation Proclamation, Letter from a Birmingham Jail.\n\nThe course will also include discussions of how the country did not always live up to the ideals on which it was based, as well as whether it has strayed from its founding principles and, if so, in what ways.\n\n                  LEARNINGN OUTCOMES\nAt the conclusion of this course students should be able to:\n1.\tIdentify and discuss the people, events and documents that influenced our founding fathers and the documents on which this country is based including the concept and source of the “Rule of Law” within the American Republic.\n2.\tCompare and contrast the Articles of Confederation and the U.S. Constitution to include the shortcomings of the former and how the latter was written to correct those deficiencies. How does the concept of dual sovereigns and federalism fit in this discussion.\n3.\tUnderstand the uniqueness of the system of government established by the U.S, Constitution and be able to broadly discuss the specific principles that make it unique. This should include the concepts found in the Bill of Rights.\n4.\tRecognize the arguments that were made to support slavery, the effect of the Dred Scott decision and how those stand up to a biblical worldview.\n5.\tDemonstrate where we as the people have failed to live up to some of the principles outlined in the Constitution, the amendments passed to correct those deficiencies and the extent they helped achieve their purpose.",
  "CM 304": "Discipleship Counseling is an integrated and comprehensive process of personal and spiritual conflict resolution through genuine repentance and faith in God. The process incorporates the presence of God in the context of a biblical world view.",
  "MAT 104": "An introduction to college mathematics is provided with an emphasis on work-world applications.  During this course you will be exposed to the basic concepts of college mathematics.  Students will perform best in this study if they have a basic understanding of high school mathematics through algebra and geometry.  The goal of the course is to build a fundamental understanding of college mathematics that provides a level of competence expected of a college graduate.  As you work through the material, you will build a useful set of skills to assist you in tasks at work and at home.  Each exercise and learning activity is designed to improve your understanding of and capabilities in applied mathematics.  The text is easy to read and the exercises are relevant to many real-life activities.  The entire study is structured to meet the needs of students in the liberal arts.",
  "PSY 213": "This course introduces the biblical principles of counseling and outlines the process by which Christian leaders can counsel people with specific problems. The course also provides an introduction to the counseling profession from a Christian perspective, explores how biblical and psychological principles may be integrated and applied to counseling issues, and describes the nature and role of the counselor as a person and as a professional.",
  "BL 500": "Hermeneutics is the science of interpretation. In this course, students will learn key interpretative techniques for engaging the Bible, from critical method to genre analysis. A component of this course will look at schools of interpretation as it relates to various movements within the historical Christian tradition and contemporary trajectories. Students will leave with a toolbox for interpreting Scripture exegetically that leads to faithful, robust biblical exposition. Students will be able to place the practical outcomes of various interpretations within contemporary Christian movements.\n\n                   Learning Outcomes\n\nThis course aims to empower and challenge students:\n\nCognitive:\n\n(1)\tTo advance towards a provisional mastery of the process of biblical interpretation understood from an evangelical Christian point of view\n\n(2)\tTo interact with and glean insight from a variety of interpretive and hermeneutical schools regarding the multifaceted nature of the Bible’s communicative stances (i.e., literary type, genre, form, etc.)\n\nPsychomotor:\n\n(3)\tTo learn and practice the interpretation of a manageable segment of the Bible which represents the expansive variety of literary types, genres and literary forms used across the whole of Scripture\n\n(4)\tTo prepare materials for the purpose of teaching the Bible to others and to suggest meaningful applications of Scripture’s meaning for a hypothetical audience\n\nAffective:\n\n(5)\tTo grow in personal conviction regarding, and growth in ability to demonstrate a.) the relevance of Scripture as the Word of God b.) the holistic nature of the Bible as a complete literary work and c.) the importance of applying biblical principles to concrete life situations in Christian obedience\n\n(6)\tTo be challenged to grow in one’s desire to listen to, and to obey God’s Word in creative\nways",
  "PSY 303": "This course provides students with foundational biblical concepts of counseling, providing practical application of principles learned through lecture, small group and whole class interaction as well as self-study and oral presentation formats. The student will ultimately be challenged to examine his/her worldview toward counseling in the light of biblical principles and how biblical foundations relate to psychological principles. [Prerequisite: Introduction to Counseling (PSY 213)]",
  "LD 201": "Although the Bible is not specifically intended to be a leadership training manual, embedded on  almost every page of the text are significant leadership concepts and examples. The focus of this\ncourse is to expose and examine many of these important principles. The intention of the course is for us to not only gain intellectual understanding, but also enable application of these ideas in  current and future leadership opportunities.\n\n                 Specific Learning Outcomes\n\nUpon completion of this course, the learner should:\n\n1.\tBe able to describe various definitions of leadership, including their own preferences.\n2.\tBe able to explain the need and call for leaders, as well as leadership motivation and preparation.\n3.\tBe able to knowledgeably choose to avoid the perils and pitfalls that have the potential to sidetrack and disqualify leaders.\n4.\tBe able to distinguish the attitudes and attributes that mark successful leaders.\n5.\tBe able to differentiate the approaches and actions that successful leaders adopt. \n6.\tBe able to analyze the costs/benefits of leadership; to make informed decisions, before accepting opportunities to lead.\n7.\tBe able to demonstrate lessons learned from the example of one or more biblical leaders. \n8.\tBe able to evaluate current personal strengths and weaknesses; to create a personal leadership profile. \n9.\tBe able to design a leadership development plan; with the intention of constructing a personal roadmap toward better leadership.",
  "PSY 313": "This course focuses on the importance of the family as the foundational biblical and social institution. Students will develop an understanding of the biblical teaching on marriage and family, skills for assessment and counseling, and resources for referring as necessary to build healthy marriages and families.",
  "PSY 323": "This course provides students with the opportunity to explore the dynamics, components, goals, and process of crisis counseling, providing practical application. This course will primarily focus on suicide crisis, crisis within family dynamics, divorce crisis, and crisis arising out of grief and loss.",
  "MAT 220": "Many employers, in today’s data-rich economy, expect their new college graduate employees to have the skills to understand and interpret data.  Students attending this course will learn statistical concepts and methods to analyze company and market data that lead to effective decision-making.  Throughout the course, students will learn both the quantitative and qualitative ways businesses use statistics from pricing and marketing to manufacturing and quality and organizational efficiency and effectiveness.",
  "PSY 333": "Students will be introduced to the basic components of conflict resolution, an invaluable tool in all aspects of life and ministry. The student will participate in role plays, practice writing agreements and discuss mediation history and theory. Emphasis will also be placed on developing and implementing a Christian framework for conflict resolution.",
  "BL 501": "Course Description:\n \nA seminar surveying major biblical themes, genres, history, and other key features of the  Hebrew Bible. This course includes a section on the development/formation of the Hebrew Bible and how the use of this text differs within the Christian tradition from other traditions, e.g., book order, book groupings, etc. Students will be able to wrestle academically and practically with the question: What is the Old Testament? \n \nLearning Outcomes :\n \nStudents will be able to: \n \n1.\tDemonstrate a mature knowledge with regard to the structure and formation of the Hebrew Bible.  \n \n2.\tDevelop and be able to articulate a learned and defensible understanding of the Hebrew Bible’s eschatological and messianic outlook, as well as its importance for the formation of some aspects of the New Testament. \n \n3.\tExplore different viewpoints regarding the Hebrew Bible’s normative status and different ways in which the Christian exegetical tradition has understood it to be authoritative for faith and practice. \n \n4.\tInterpret a cross section, theme or set of passages in the Hebrew Bible in light of an understanding of its overall eschatological and messianic outlook.",
  "BL 502": "This course will engage deeply with a particular book of the Old Testament. The book will not necessarily be the same each term. The purpose is to learn how to analyze a single text, how to engage with current interpretations of said text, and offer one’s own interpretation applied to a local congregation. Students will learn how to place that interpretation within the larger contexts of the Bible and various Christian movements.",
  "BL 550": "This course is for learning the practical use of biblical software for teaching and preaching. Basic Bible study skills will be surveyed and implemented. Students will learn elementary vocabulary and grammar. Students will learn to use a Bible software, a lexicon for accurate word studies, and how to develop a teaching/preaching outline.",
  "BL 561": "Course Description:\n\nA language class focused on learning the basics of Hebrew vocabulary and grammar as utilized in the day-to-day ministry of a local congregation. The emphasis for this course will be learning to utilize biblical software to aid in applying the languages to ministry: preaching, teaching, and discipling.\n\nLearning Outcomes:\n\n1.\tA general understanding of the workings of the biblical languages and their impact on hermeneutics and Scripture exegesis. \n2.\tImportant awareness of Scripture&#039;s original meaning. Concepts for translation and knowledge to further investigate and apply the Bible to a variety of modern circumstances into the original significance of Scripture.\n3.\tThe ability to preach, teach, lead, and shepherd the congregation of the local congregation, help in spiritual maturity, and concern for non-Christians in the ministry of the Word of God in the hearts and lives both churched and unchurched.",
  "BL 562": "Course Description \n \nA language class focused on learning the basics of Hebrew vocabulary and grammar as utilized in the day-to-day ministry of a local congregation. This course will emphasize learning to utilize biblical software to aid in applying the languages to ministry: preaching, teaching, and discipling. \n \n                           Learning Outcomes \n \n1.\tA general understanding of the workings of the biblical languages and their impact on hermeneutics and Scripture exegesis.  \n2.\tImportant awareness of Scripture&#039;s original meaning. Concepts for, and knowledge to further investigate and apply Bible to a variety of modern circumstances into, the original significance of Scripture. \n3.\tThe ability of preach, teach, lead and shepherd the congregation of the local congregation, help in spiritual maturity, and concern for non-Christians in the ministry of the Word of God in the hearts and lives both churched and unchurched.",
  "PSY 343": "This course is designed to challenge scholars to seek a place of intimacy with the Holy Spirit and to regard the Holy Spirit as the actual Counselor as we seek to minister to those in need.  Guided by the Holy Spirit through Scripture and the insights of godly persons who have studied the doctrine of the Holy Spirit, scholars will grow to understand, appreciate, and apply this aspect of the role of the Holy Spirit in counseling settings.  \n\nThis course will address the person and work of the Holy Spirit, as well as the Holy Spirit&#039;s power, gifts, roles, and fruit as they relate to biblical counseling.\n\nThis course will engage scholars in a wide scope of Christian exercises and practices aimed at broadening and deepening their relationship with God, as well as understanding a biblical view of self and others. As dependence on and the relationship to God’s Spirit broadens and deepens and as the discerning and decision-making skills mature, scholars will be learning to make choices with biblical confidence and clarity.",
  "BL 505": "A key component of a full comprehension of the New Testament in light of the Old Testament is the history comprising the time between the two testaments, or the intertestamental period. This course will cover major intertestamental texts, major intertestamental events, e.g. Maccabean Revolt, and broader world historical events such as the Hellenization of the world as a preparation for Messiah. Part of this course should examine how the Bible understood as canon differs from the works produced in the intertestamental period.",
  "BL 512": "A seminar surveying major biblical themes, genres, history, and other key features of the Christian New Testament. This course includes a section on the development/formation of the Christian New Testament with emphasis on dating, methodology, and canonization. Students will be able to wrestle academically and practically with the question: What is the New Testament? An extensive examination of the person and work of Jesus through the Gospels, the early development of the church through the work of the apostles, and the apocalyptic literature of Revelation.",
  "BL 551": "A language class focused on learning the basics of Greek vocabulary and grammar as utilized in the day-to-day ministry of a local congregation. An emphasis for this course will be learning to utilize biblical software to aid in applying the languages to ministry. This course includes the history of textual traditions and textual families.",
  "BL 552": "[Greek exegesis] This seminar goes beyond the basics of Greek in order to learn how interpret and translate the Bible from the original languages. The emphasis for this course will be learning advanced tools of Greek for the purposes of teaching and preaching. As a part of this course, either an entire New Testament book (e.g. 1 John, 2 Peter, etc.) or at least a substantial portion of a New Testament book (e.g. Sermon on the Mount, Romans 1-5) will be translated.",
  "ICL 510": "This course is a study of the theology of evangelism, missions, and the eternal destiny of humankind. In addition, contemporary and global issues in the theology of missions and outreach will be considered. The theological foundation established through this study will form a basis for developing a theologically-motivated philosophy of ministry that has a clear goal of impacting the unreached peoples of the world.",
  "ICL 520": "This course is a study of the theology of leadership, vocation, and work as they relate to providing leadership in diverse ministry contexts. Students will be prepared to reflect biblically and theologically on leadership, vocation, and work in order to make wise personal decisions and to help others understand the relationship between work, ministry, and service in the Kingdom of God.",
  "OL 531": "Special combinations of OL 530 and OL 540 for MABS, MACM, &amp; MATS majors.\nThis course is designed to create an understanding of organizational culture and the role organizational leadership plays in managing significant and successful change. Every organization’s culture is distinct, and this course intends to guide students in effective analysis of an organization’s culture for the purpose of managing the culture toward desired behavioral change and goal achievement. The format of the course will be that of a &quot;leadership laboratory&quot; in which the students participate in developing and executing each step of a rigorous change process that includes analyzing the culture of an organization (both subjectively and objectively), determining the need for the change, the strategy of and planning for the change, and the execution of the change from start to completion.",
  "BUS 450": "This course will introduce several areas of legal responsibility, risk, and exposure to small and medium-sized businesses.  Students will learn how to select and work with an attorney and form and work with a board of directors.  Exposure to operational liability, insurance, and bankruptcy protection will equip students to recognize and mitigate business risk.  Students will also study the processes of buying and selling a business entity.  Several guest lecturers will address essential topics surrounding contract law, employment law, and business formations.\n\n                      Learning Outcomes\nUpon successful completion of this course, the student will be able to:\n1. Demonstrate an understanding of the Legal Environment of Business.\n2. Apply basic legal knowledge to business transactions.\n3. Communicate effectively using standard business and legal terminology.\n\nINSTRUCTOR OFFICE HOURS:",
  "BL 513": "This course will engage deeply with a particular book of the New Testament. The book will not necessarily be the same each term. The purpose is to learn how to analyze a single text, how to engage with current interpretations of said text, offer one’s own interpretation of the text for a local congregation. Students will learn how to place that interpretation within the larger contexts of the Bible and various Christian movements.",
  "ICL 500": "A study of biblical principles and methods for personal awareness, spiritual growth, character formation, discipleship, and equipping Christian believers for leadership in global business and non-profit organizations. The goal of this course is to equip students in spiritual formation practices in the context of intercultural leadership.",
  "ICL 530": "This course will prepare students to analyze the social and cultural factors influencing people’s behavior and development in an era of globalization. Students will be equipped to analyze global contexts for the purpose of connecting with local people and meeting their needs.",
  "ACCT 311": "An overview of accounting from the perspective of a non-accounting manager in order to provide that manager with the tools to understand the essentials of how finance functions within the organization including internal controls, the function of accounting and the difference between historical accounting data and financial planning.\n\n                      Learning Outcomes\nUpon successful completion of this course, the student will be able to:\n1.\tCorrectly apply terminology used in accounting, which is the language of business.\n2.\tAnalyze business transactions to record these business transactions in the accounting records according to generally accepted accounting principles.\n3.\tDemonstrate his/her ability to complete the accounting cycle for both service and merchandise entities.\n4.\tCompile and analyze the four basic financial statements—Income Statement, Statement of Owner’s Equity, Balance Sheet, and Statement of Cash Flows—according to generally accepted accounting principles.\n5.\tAnalyze and record transactions related to stocks, bonds, and investments.\n6.\tIntegrate biblical principles related to financial accounting.",
  "ICL 540": "This course is designed for students who plan to communicate across cultural barriers, both within North America as well as those who plan a career in a global setting. Verbal and nonverbal factors facilitate or impede effective communication in every encounter between persons of different cultures. The goal of this course is to prepare students to discern these factors and to develop skills that mitigate cultural barriers in communication, as well as excellent communication skills in an intercultural or global context.",
  "ICL 550": "Students will understand various components of multicultural teams such as how to build trust and resolve cross-cultural conflict. Emphasis is placed on building collective unity around a common vision and the leader’s development of qualities and skills necessary to navigate a multicultural team.",
  "ICL 560": "This course is designed to equip the leader to understand, assess, and lead in a wide variety of cultural contexts including a wide variety of rural, suburban, and urban worlds, as well as complex cross-generational and ethnic settings.",
  "OL 565": "This course will explore the various risks that businesses face daily. Dealing with Business Risk (BR) can be challenging for those who do not have a reliable moral and ethical foundation; hence, a Biblical basis for evaluating BR is emphasized throughout the course. Among the topics studied are contract law, insurance risk (warranties and indemnification), human resources and employment law, corporate finance, cybersecurity, operational risks, etc. Managing the tension between risk, innovation, and investing in various organizations will prepare the student for successful managerial leadership.",
  "OL 570": "Course Description\n\nThis course examines the fundamental techniques of financial statement analysis and their application to strategic planning and decision-making. The course covers the analysis and interpretation of financial information, including the analysis of balance sheets, income statements, and statements of cash flows in both vocational and personal environments.",
  "BUS 301/MIS 300": "PLEASE NOTE: This class will NOT be meeting on campus 10/7, 10/21, or 10/28.\n\nA comprehensive guide to the theory and practice of Business as Mission. This course explores conceptual foundations for understanding BAM’s place in global mission and equips students with practical knowledge for implementing kingdom-strategic business ventures.",
  "ECON 301": "The primary purpose of this course is to introduce you to the social science of economics and to examine this discipline&#039;s basic assumptions, methods, and models. Essential concepts such as supply, demand, and market equilibrium provide a sound basis for analysis. We will also be exploring cost and benefit evaluations, gross domestic product, money, business cycles, government policies, and international trade, as well as economics’ role in today’s organizations and our personal lives.",
  "ICL 600": "This course examines management principles and practice for nonprofit organizations. Primary focus is given to Christ-focused leadership in a nonprofit environment, the motivation of staff and volunteers, the role of the founder and the board, and types and structures of nonprofit organizations. Additional focus is placed on interpersonal skills such as managing stress, problem solving, coaching and motivation of others, organizational culture as well as conflict resolution utilizing foundational Christian principles.",
  "ICL 610": "This course examines the process of building community relationships and helping communities analyze their context, identify priorities and develop a plan of action for addressing key issues. The use of participatory community surveys, focus group discussions, project proposal writing, monitoring, budgeting, and sustainability will be discussed. Learning how to apply principles of servant leadership and demonstrate a Christian witness throughout the process is emphasized in this course.",
  "ICL 620": "This course examines systemic issues such as poverty, racism, and justice as they apply to Gospel Ministry in urban centers around the globe. Students will learn about relational discipleship and community engagement with the goal of transformative interaction with residents, multi-ethnic ministry and urban apologetics.",
  "CT 525": "An intensive, systematic approach to the study of Theology. This course will cover the doctrine of the Bible; Theology Proper (God the Father, Trinity); Angels, Satan, Demons; Man and Sin; Christ, Salvation, Holy Spirit, Church and Future Events",
  "CT 590": "This course is a special seminar that will consider the particular opportunities and challenges of leading at international government, corporate, and church levels.  Leaders from these levels will predominate the course as guest speakers. The course examines strategic aspects of managing a global or multinational business or ministry, with particular focus on cultural dynamics, legal and political considerations, and competitive market forces. International location TBD between Israel, Belgium/Europe.\n\nFollowing the journeys of the Apostle Paul through the book of Acts and his various letters, this course is designed to teach biblical theology through the context of applied onsite learning at various Biblical sites in Turkey, Greece and Rome. Lessons in bible and theology will be taught through instruction, discussion, visits and exploration of historic sites, and extensive onsite study.",
  "ICL 630": "This course introduces students macro- and microeconomic theories relevant to understanding today&#039;s global economy. Emphasis is placed on the interplay between economic, cultural, legal, and political systems and their impact on both national well-being and international business strategy. Students will also explore foundational rules of international law governing transactions and their influence on intercultural leadership and policy-making.",
  "ICL 640": "This course examines poverty and health-related issues as they intersect with cultural, theological, and global realities. Students will explore the causes and consequences of poverty and development. The course will equip students to develop practical, biblically faithful strategies for transformational ministry among the poor.",
  "LD 289": "NOTE: This course Zoom link is shared with MGT 489 Capstone due to a shortage of Zoom room availability. Please contact the office if a conflict between the two capstone project meetings arises.\\\\",
  "BL 599": "Topic to be selected by student which will pertain to a particular opportunity or problem in their organization or ministry. The aim is to show scholarly research and deep understanding of the key concepts learned in the program. The purpose of the Capstone is to help the student apply and demonstrate the program goals taught throughout the master’s level programs. The development of this project will be conducted throughout the programs and will be guided by a professor in the programs.  \n\n\n                       Learning Outcomes\n  \n1.\tStudents will demonstrate scholarly research by engaging with the best contemporary research on the topic.   \n\n2.\tStudents will demonstrate the ability to understand, historically locate, and integrate historical texts showing the development of the idea through key thinkers.\n\n3.\tStudents will demonstrate understanding of the key concepts learned in the program relevant to the topic by drawing proper inferences and integrating these ideas.    \n\n4.\tStudents will demonstrate the ability to make a valid/strong and sound/cogent argument for a thesis while also rebutting/undercutting relevant defeaters to that thesis.",
  "ICL 660": "This capstone course is an integration of previous course work and personal research. Students work with advisors to propose and conduct an integrated research study that will enhance an area of interest and contribute to existing research in the chosen field. Closely tied to the research is the student’s personal vision statement, composed during the course Spiritual Formation for Missional Leaders.",
  "LD 471": "Course Description\n\nWhat is leadership? How can you develop as a leader? This course explores this expansive topic not only covering several leadership theories but also attempting to provide practical ideas on becoming and developing as a leader. We look at how academics attempt to explain the nature of leadership through various models. We also study this important topic from a realistic and applicable perspective, drawing on Biblical characters and current leaders from business, politics, and the news.\n\n                     Learning Outcomes\n\nUpon completion of the course, the learner should be able to:\n\n1.\tDescribe the theory of Servant Leadership (Blanchard/Greenleaf)\n\n2.\tUnderstand Dr. John Maxwell’s 21 Laws of Leadership, concentrating on at least three\n\n3.\tCreate a personal mission statement per the Covey Mission Statement Builder (with presentation)\n\n4.\tDevelop a knowledge of the various leadership theories highlighted the in Northouse textbook\n\n5.\tPresent a leadership case orally, highlighting leadership styles and skills (with PowerPoint)"
};

